            {
                "entry_id": "c1e71ce0-fcec-32ed-b0db-6cac64ee7a94",
                "word": "underwood",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "f3fc1da7-6b0a-4c8d-b5b0-de964e5d2535",
                        "entry_id": "c1e71ce0-fcec-32ed-b0db-6cac64ee7a94",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "underwoods"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b5e6e302-306e-3f36-8e60-22a6e989dcc7",
                        "entry_id": "c1e71ce0-fcec-32ed-b0db-6cac64ee7a94",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Underbrush, undergrowth."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3ed5d6ce-2afe-4245-ba0c-8ddaca6ef322",
                                "sense_id": "b5e6e302-306e-3f36-8e60-22a6e989dcc7",
                                "text": "1670, John Evelyn, Sylva, or A Discourse of Forest-Trees and the Propagation of Timber, London, Chapter 3, Of the Oak, pp. 16-17,\nWhat improvement the stirring of the ground about the roots of Oaks is to the Trees I have already hinted; and yet in Copses where they stand warm, and so thickn’d with the under wood, as this culture cannot be practis’d, they prove in time to be goodly Trees."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "22b7c441-74d7-4e35-9a54-f7204b7e4ac9",
                                "sense_id": "b5e6e302-306e-3f36-8e60-22a6e989dcc7",
                                "text": "[…] the Country near the Sea-side, and some few Miles further is full of short Under-wood, and thorny Shrubs, which tore our Cloaths to Rags […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b4a87050-37eb-4269-afdd-04012adf520d",
                                "sense_id": "b5e6e302-306e-3f36-8e60-22a6e989dcc7",
                                "text": "‘Accordingly I hid myself in some thick underwood, determining to devote the ensuing hours to reflection on my situation.’"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0d76c2d2-9b2b-48aa-a9af-ea10b59ef037",
                                "sense_id": "b5e6e302-306e-3f36-8e60-22a6e989dcc7",
                                "text": "But tenants may cut underwood, and take wood sufficient to repair the pales, hedges, and fences, and what is called by law plough-bote, fire-bote, and other house-bote."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "12e53898-db70-41f7-ba8d-38766b4a2253",
                                "sense_id": "b5e6e302-306e-3f36-8e60-22a6e989dcc7",
                                "text": "In cutting coppices the usual plan is to leave what is termed plashers where the underwoods are thin and require new wood; a far better plan is to plant young stuff, which is not only cheaper but makes much better underwoods."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "2b0ffa99-66a7-3338-9783-fde863bbcf1b",
